  "topic": "Vtable Layout and Dynamic Dispatch Mechanism in C++",
  "explanation": "In the C++ object model, virtual functions are implemented via a vtable (virtual table), which is a compiler-generated table of function pointers. Each polymorphic class with virtual functions has its own vtable, and each object of such a class contains an implicit pointer (vptr) to its class's vtable. This mechanism enables dynamic (runtime) dispatch of virtual function calls, allowing the program to invoke the correct function implementation based on the actual object type, even when accessed through a base class pointer. Understanding the internal layout of vtables and vptrs is crucial for grasping how C++ achieves runtime polymorphism efficiently and how the compiler manages function call resolution internally.",
  "syntax": "For a class with virtual functions:\n\n// Class definition\nclass ClassName {\npublic:\n    virtual void func();\n    // ... other members ...\n};\n\n// Memory layout for an object of ClassName:\n// [vptr (pointer to vtable)] + data members\n// The vtable is an array of function pointers generated by the compiler.\n\n// Virtual function call at runtime:\n// * vptr is dereferenced to locate the vtable\n// * Function pointer is retrieved from vtable based on function's offset\n// * Function is invoked via the function pointer\n",
